Subject: ARM: dts: meson8b: add the NAND controller and its pins

The NFC sits at 0xd0048600, which is offset 0x48600 inside the existing
apb bus@d0000000 (ranges = <0x0 0xd0000000 0x200000>).

CLKID_NAND is the register-interface gate on HHI_GCLK_MPEG1 bit 24 and
has to be listed, or clk_disable_unused() switches the controller's
registers off at late_initcall; CLKID_NAND_CLK is the top of the
functional tree.

No "interrupts" property. The controller has a ready/busy interrupt, but
no Amlogic code has ever enabled it on this SoC family and there is no
in-tree, out-of-tree or vendor device tree from which the GIC number
could be taken and no board on which it has been seen to fire. The
driver waits for ready in software on meson8/8b and does not request an
interrupt at all, so a number here would buy nothing and would risk
claiming another peripheral's line. Anyone wanting to establish it can
add the property in a local .dts and boot with meson_nand.use_irq_rb=1.

assigned-clock-parents does less than it looks like it does, and the
comment says so: nand_clk_sel is a 3-bit mux of which the clock driver
models only settings 0..4, so this recovers from a bootloader that left
it at 5..7, but nand_clk_sel carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT and not
CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, so the driver's first clk_set_rate()
reparents away from fclk_div4 again.

Both nodes are disabled/unreferenced, so no existing meson8b board is
affected. Boards that want the controller must also disable &sdhc:
BOOT_0..BOOT_10 carry the NAND bus and the sdxc_c bus on the same pads.

Rebase note for downstreams: applied on top of Armbian's meson-6.12
series, the pinctrl hunk lands with a line offset (0028/0059/0063/0064
and the onecloud dtsi patches insert nodes above it) and Armbian's
patching tool records the patch as needing a rebase. That is a log
annotation, not a failure -- both hunks still match their context --
but the patch is not anchored against that series and one more inserted
node in the cbus pinctrl block could turn the offset into a reject.

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/amlogic/meson8b.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/amlogic/meson8b.dtsi
index 2d9d24d..acffd1c 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/amlogic/meson8b.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/amlogic/meson8b.dtsi
@@ -257,6 +257,51 @@
 		#size-cells = <1>;
 		ranges = <0x0 0xd0000000 0x200000>;
 
+		nfc: nand-controller@48600 {
+			compatible = "amlogic,meson8b-nfc";
+			reg = <0x48600 0x100>;
+			reg-names = "nfc";
+			/*
+			 * No "interrupts" here on purpose. The controller does
+			 * have a ready/busy interrupt line, but no Amlogic
+			 * code has ever enabled it on this SoC family -- their
+			 * driver sets NAND_CTRL_NONE_RB unconditionally and
+			 * ships the request_irq() commented out -- so there is
+			 * no in-tree, out-of-tree or vendor device tree from
+			 * which the GIC number could be taken and no board on
+			 * which it has been observed to fire. The driver waits
+			 * for ready in software here and does not request an
+			 * interrupt at all, so an unverified number would buy
+			 * nothing and would risk claiming another peripheral's
+			 * line. Add one locally if you want to measure it.
+			 *
+			 * CLKID_NAND is the register-interface gate on
+			 * HHI_GCLK_MPEG1; without it listed here
+			 * clk_disable_unused() switches the controller's
+			 * registers off at late_initcall. CLKID_NAND_CLK is
+			 * the top of the functional tree.
+			 */
+			clocks = <&clkc CLKID_NAND>,
+				 <&clkc CLKID_NAND_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "core", "device";
+			/*
+			 * nand_clk_sel is a 3-bit mux of which the clock
+			 * driver models only settings 0..4, so a bootloader
+			 * that left it at 5..7 would orphan the clock at
+			 * probe. This only sanitises that: nand_clk_sel
+			 * carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT and not
+			 * CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, so the driver's first
+			 * clk_set_rate() reparents away from fclk_div4 again
+			 * -- do not expect to find fclk_div4 in
+			 * /sys/kernel/debug/clk/nand_clk_sel later on.
+			 */
+			assigned-clocks = <&clkc CLKID_NAND_SEL>;
+			assigned-clock-parents = <&clkc CLKID_FCLK_DIV4>;
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
 		mali: gpu@c0000 {
 			compatible = "amlogic,meson8b-mali", "arm,mali-450";
 			reg = <0xc0000 0x40000>;
@@ -498,6 +543,32 @@
 			};
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * The same BOOT bank pads as sdxc_c_pins above: BOOT_0..7 are
+		 * both nand_io and sdxc_d0/d13/d47_c, BOOT_8 is both
+		 * nand_io_ce0 and sdxc_clk_c, BOOT_10 is both nand_io_rb0 and
+		 * sdxc_cmd_c. A board may therefore enable &nfc or &sdhc, but
+		 * never both.
+		 *
+		 * nand_io_ce1 is left out because the driver hardcodes the
+		 * ready/busy interrupt to CE0, and the DQS groups because DQS
+		 * only exists in the toggle/sync modes this driver does not
+		 * drive.
+		 */
+		nand_pins: nand {
+			mux {
+				groups = "nand_io",
+					 "nand_io_ce0",
+					 "nand_io_rb0",
+					 "nand_ale",
+					 "nand_cle",
+					 "nand_wen_clk",
+					 "nand_ren_clk";
+				function = "nand";
+				bias-disable;
+			};
+		};
+
 		pwm_c1_pins: pwm-c1 {
 			mux {
 				groups = "pwm_c1";
